.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_CREATE 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_create \- create a fixed-size memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool *ccol_mempool_create(size_t elem_count,
                        size_t elem_size,
                        bool fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
                        bool single_threaded,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool that manages a fixed number of fixed-size elements,
so every allocation returns an element of the same size. The pool uses a
free list to give O(1) allocation and deallocation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
elem_count                 Number of elements in the pool (must be > 0)
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes. It must be > 0.
                          The minimum is sizeof(uintptr_t). The maximum
                          is SIZE_MAX less the stride rounding
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from the heap when the pool
                          is empty
single_threaded           If true, do not lock (faster but not
                          thread-safe)
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL for the
                          default
err                       Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The stride between elements is elem_size rounded up to 16 bytes, and the
library then rounds it further up to a power of two unless it was built
with -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1 (see doc/building.md in the source
tree). In both cases every element, not only the first one, is aligned
for any object type. This matches what malloc() guarantees, so any
object that you can store in heap memory can go into an entry
If elem_size < sizeof(uintptr_t), the library rounds it up
Returns NULL with an error in two cases: an elem_size that would
overflow size_t after the stride rounding, and an elem_count and a
stride that together would overflow size_t. A pool holds one stride for
each element, then one status byte for each element, all in one block
With the fallback on, an allocation from the pool never fails until the
system runs out of memory
The caller must destroy the pool with ccol_mempool_destroy()
The pool calls the functions of mmgmt_procs for its dynamic fallback
entries, and for its own bookkeeping of them, in the middle of an update
of its own state, with its lock held when it has one. Those functions
must therefore not call back into this same pool: with a lock the call
deadlocks, and without one it corrupts that state. An allocator built on
a pool must draw from a different pool

Sizing for performance:

Give a thread-safe pool at least 8 elements. Below that there is no room
for a cache for each thread, so every allocation and every free takes
the lock of the pool and concurrent callers serialise against one
another. 8 is a floor, not a target: the cache size grows with the
pool, so a pool sized for real use does much better than one sized at
exactly 8.

A pool gives out at most one cache for every 8 elements, and at most 16
in all. A thread that arrives when every cache is taken works through
the lock of the pool (one lock for each allocation and each free, which
is what a pool without a cache costs) until a thread that holds a cache
exits, at which point it gets one. Size a pool that many threads share
so that each of them gets a cache.

A pool that receives a cache allocates a reserve above elem_count, which
keeps elem_count available to any thread no matter how many entries
other threads hold in their own caches. The reserve costs memory: at
most half of elem_count, falling to a quarter at 8192 elements, an
eighth at 16384, and a few percent or less from about 65536 upward. The
reserve stops growing once a cache reaches its maximum depth, so a large
pool pays proportionally far less than a small one.

ccol_mempool_total_capacity() reports elem_count, and
ccol_mempool_used_count() counts only the entries that the pool truly
handed out, not an entry that the pool merely pulled into a cache. What
the reserve does change is how exact the upper end of that count is
under concurrent load. An entry leaves the cache of a thread with no
lock held, and so with no count to consult, which means entries go into
a cache before anybody knows whether they are needed. That decision
favours the guarantee above; in exchange, concurrent callers can hold a
few entries more than elem_count at one time, for a short time, but
never more than elem_count plus the reserve. In either case the memory
is allocated when the pool is created, so nothing grows at run time.
See ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3).

A thread gets a cache only once it holds a value on a thread-specific
key of the library, which is what lets the library free that cache when
the thread exits. A thread for which that fails works through the lock
of the pool, as above, and asks again at its next miss. A process that
has no thread-specific key left to give, and a library that is being
unloaded, give no pool a cache.

Neither a pool created with single_threaded = true nor a pool created by
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() ever uses the cache, and
the reserve and the 8-element floor do not apply to them.

The right elem_count, elem_size and single_threaded setting depend on
three things: the access pattern, the number of threads, and the object
lifetime of the program that uses the pool. No single choice is best
for all of them, so measure rather than assume. "make bench" in the
source tree runs the benchmarks of the pool beside the system allocator,
on the current machine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when single_threaded is false, in which case the pool uses a
mutex.

On the common path a thread-safe pool takes no lock at all: each thread
keeps a small private cache of entries and reaches the shared free list
only to refill or flush that cache. Threads that allocate and free at
the same time therefore do not contend with one another, and throughput
rises as you add threads.

An entry in the cache of one thread returns to the shared free list when
that thread exits, and a destroy of the pool frees its whole block,
those entries included. The allocation of the cache itself is a
separate matter; ccol_mempool_destroy(3) gives the points at which that
allocation goes back to your allocator. Join a thread before you destroy
a pool that it used. That is enough, because the cleanup of a thread
runs before pthread_join() returns.

A single_threaded = true pool has no lock and no cache. Never use such a
pool from more than one thread.
.fi
